---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getChapters, chapterHref } from '../lib/chapters';

const chapters = await getChapters();
const first = chapters[0];
---

<Base title="About" currentPage="about">
  <article class="page">
    <p class="eyebrow">About this site</p>
    <h1 class="chapter-title">Why this book exists</h1>
    <p class="deck">
      Most explanations of computers start in the middle. This one starts before electricity and does not skip.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <em>Signal to Thought</em> is a short book about how we got from signal fires to machines that write. It is
        written for curious adults with no technical background, and for technical people who learned the pieces out
        of order and would like to see them line up.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rule for every chapter is the same: one new idea, made concrete, resting only on the chapters before it.
        A chapter about attention in a language model should be readable by someone whose last stop was a chapter
        about relays, provided they walked the chapters in between. Where a word like <em>bit</em>, <em>gate</em>, or
        <em>gradient</em> appears, it has been built first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hardware and software are told together, because they were never really separate. The shape of the machine
        decided which programs were easy to write, and the programs people wanted decided which machines got built.
        That back-and-forth is the plot.
      </p>
      <h2>How to read it</h2>
      <p>
        In order, ideally. The sidebar lists the Parts and their chapters, and the thin line down its edge shows how
        far you have come. Each chapter ends with a short note on what to carry forward and a card for the next one.
        Many chapters include a small interactive in a "Try it" panel. They run in the page, need no setup, and are
        there to be poked at.
      </p>
      <h2>Status</h2>
      <p>
        This is a work in progress. Chapters marked as not yet written have their place in the book and a short
        description of where they are headed. The writing and the analogies are original to this project.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={chapterHref(first)}>Start reading →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
